{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ aging workplace issues older workers)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1443857052#6_3252333923","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1954","rank":1,"snippet":"Laura Anderko, RN, PhD; Jason S. Roffenbender, MS; Ron Z. Goetzel, PhD; Francois Millard; Kevin Wildenhaus, PhD; Charles DeSantis; William Novelli, MA Abstract Background Workforce Trends and Wellness The <cover>Workplace as a Microcosm of Society Worker Wellness and the ACA Bridging Work and Wellness: Lessons Learned Nontraditional Settings for Worksite Wellness Effectiveness of Incentives Improving Health Through the Power of Community Conclusion Acknowledgments Author Information References \" , \"segment\": \"The ACA was designed to address 4 key prevention areas: 1) community prevention, 2) clinical prevention, 3) public health infrastructure and training, and 4) research and surveillance focused on workforce wellness. Understanding key issues that affect the American workforce is critical to improving prevention efforts (9). Top of Page Workforce Trends and Wellness The health of the modern workforce is changing. The aging of the labor force contributes to the complexity of health needs; chronic disease has become a significant concern for employers, due to escalating health care costs. The group of workers aged 55 or older, </cover>which comprised 13% of the labor force in 2000, is projected to increase to 20% by 2020 and account for 19% of the labor force by 2050 (1,10). Employees "}}
