{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1154622543#9_2077761899","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is focused on Baby Boomers/aging workforce impacts on employers and directly identifies workplace issues such as lack of strategic planning for retirements, potential skills gaps, need to retain workers, brain drain, and adapting to a multigenerational workforce.","learned":"Aging-workplace issues include Baby Boomer retirements creating skills gaps and brain drain, employer need for forecasting and succession planning, retention programs, and adjustment to a multigenerational workforce."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1974","reason":"This query produced a strong aging-workforce employer-focused result about retirement, skills gaps, and planning; continue for more dedicated evidence in this result set."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1974"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1154622543#2_2077747599","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1974","rank":2,"snippet":"there be a Baby Boomer brain drain? What impact will Boomers who remain in the workforce have? How will Boomers affect the career prospects of younger workers? Navigating a multi-generational workplace: Is your business prepared for Boomers who don’t plan to retire? Companies must be welcoming to an <cover>aging workforce: Employers must adjust to a new workforce dynamic: \" , \"segment\": \"AARP published a study in June 2011, showing that 76 million Boomers would be facing retirement in the future. However, when Boomers in the workforce were asked about their intentions, 41% responded that they did not want to quit working. Will there be a Baby Boomer brain drain? Prior to the Great Recession, a major concern for businesses was the coming brain drain, which would be caused by Boomers retiring in droves from the workplace, and taking with them their vast technical skills, </cover>historical knowledge, and honed industry-specific abilities. A question now is, will this brain drain still play out as predicted? It appears now that many Boomers will stay in the workforce longer as a result of the recession and the slow economic recovery. Researchers are finding that these trends vary "}}
