{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1440314704#0_3246487112","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is a NIOSH/CDC page dedicated to 'Productive Aging and Work' and directly explains workplace aging issues from a life-span perspective, including that aging involves both losses and gains, physical stamina may decline, accumulated knowledge may increase, and workplaces should be designed so workers of all ages can perform optimally.","learned":"Aging-related workplace issues include changing worker capacities over the life span, balancing declines in physical stamina with gains in knowledge, and designing workplaces for optimal performance across age groups."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1978","reason":"The NIOSH productive-aging result set is producing authoritative grade-3 evidence on aging and workplace design, health, and safety; continue to collect more dedicated documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1978"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1890256619#0_3026990165","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1978","rank":4,"snippet":"Safety Signs Seen by Everybody? BY GRAPHIC PRODUCTS STAFF Our society has become inundated with images of safety signs—on public transportation, in the workplace, in stores, on products, on equipment, and so much more. Unfortunately, these warning, danger, and general safety signs are not always easy to read and contain so much information that they are often not understood or simply ignored. Additionally, are the signs understood by the exact populations who need to understand them? As more baby boomers in the workforce get <cover>older, creating signs that can be easily read is a big concern. According to a March 2016 NIOSH Productive Aging and Work article , “Today, one in every five American workers </cover>is over 65, and in 2020, one in four American workers will be over 55.” More than ever, it is important to create safety signs that will reach the more mature workforce’s needs. Safety Sign Design Elements A useful safety sign is seen, read, and understood when it is needed most.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Are Your Safety Signs Seen by Everybody? | Graphic Products\" , \"url\": \""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"34/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1890256619#0_3026990165","relevance":2,"reason":"The article directly discusses a concrete aging-workplace safety issue: as more Baby Boomers remain in the workforce, safety signs need to be readable and understandable for older workers. However, it is mainly about safety-sign design rather than being dedicated broadly to aging and workplace issues.","learned":"Aging-related workplace issues include the need to adapt safety communication and signage for older workers' visual and comprehension needs."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1978","reason":"The NIOSH productive-aging query has produced several authoritative grade-3 results and the current result remains relevant; continue to see if more dedicated productive-aging-at-work documents appear."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1978"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1890256619#1_3026991830","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1978","rank":5,"snippet":": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1890256619#1_3026991830\" , \"end_char\": 1583.000000 , \"headings\": \"Are Your Safety Signs Seen by Everybody? Are Your Safety Signs Seen by Everybody? Safety Sign Design Elements Text Images Color Goals for Safety Signs Be Seen and Read \" , \"segment\": \"Additionally, are the signs understood by the exact populations who need to understand them? As more baby boomers in the workforce get <cover>older, creating signs that can be easily read is a big concern. According to a March 2016 NIOSH Productive Aging and Work article , “Today, one in every five American workers </cover>is over 65, and in 2020, one in four American workers will be over 55.” More than ever, it is important to create safety signs that will reach the more mature workforce’s needs. Safety Sign Design Elements A useful safety sign is seen, read, and understood when it is needed most. It causes people to act in a manner that will keep them safe. Good design strategically uses text, images, and color to ensure that safety signs can be easily identified, read, and understood. "}}
